{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"24b32ebe-b744-45d6-92da-101b5b82bcb4","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The generator has been updated to fix the heart rate corridor clamping issue, and the explanation has been written to [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-N0I6cM/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n1. **Fix in [`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-N0I6cM/gen.py#L10-L13)**:\n   In `hr_target()`, changed the lower bound property name from `\"value\"` to `\"start\"`:\n   ```python\n   def hr_target(step):\n       \"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete's LTHR.\"\"\"\n       lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n       return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n   ```\n   - **Why**: Intervals.icu treats `{value, end}` as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target that causes Garmin to clamp corridors to the watch's local zones. Using `{start, end}` instructs Intervals to export an Instant-HR corridor using the athlete's Intervals LTHR threshold, rendering exact raw bpm corridors on the watch.\n\n2. **Colleague's note breakdown** recorded in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-N0I6cM/ANSWER.md):\n   - **Applied**: Using `start` instead of `value` for the HR target lower bound, while keeping `units: \"%lthr\"`.\n   - **Ignored**: Top-level text `description` formatting for the Intervals web UI parser, and manual reproduction/sync instructions involving external APIs.\n","duration_seconds":37.142048,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":147298,"output_tokens":1622,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":148920}}}
